# What is mAIcelium

mAIcelium is a multi-IDE, multi-project AI workspace orchestration framework.
Its goal is to let multiple AI agents (Claude Code, Cursor, Antigravity) collaborate
on multiple software projects simultaneously, with a single source of truth for
AI configuration.

## Core idea

A central repository acts as the **control plane**:
- It holds all AI rules, skills, commands, prompts, and MCP definitions under `mesh/`.
- Real project repositories connect as **symlinks** under `projects/`.
- IDE-specific dotfolders (`.cursor/`, `.claude/`, `.agents/`) are auto-generated
  mirrors of `mesh/` — they are never edited directly.

```
mAIcelium/
├── mesh/              ← Single source of truth for all AI config
│   ├── rules/         ← Rules injected into every agent context
│   ├── skills/        ← Operational playbooks agents can follow
│   ├── commands/      ← Slash commands + Python scripts
│   ├── prompts/       ← Reusable prompt templates
│   └── mcp/           ← MCP server definitions (IDE-agnostic)
├── projects/          ← Symlinks to real project repos on disk
├── repos/
│   └── _registry.yaml ← Index of all available repos (check before /add_project)
├── bin/               ← Workspace management scripts
│   ├── init.py           ← One-time workspace setup
│   ├── add_project.py    ← Link a new project repo
│   ├── remove_project.py ← Unlink a project repo
│   ├── sync_symlinks.py  ← Regenerate all dotfolder mirrors + MCP configs
│   ├── separate_git.py   ← Move .git out of workspace root (separated git mode)
│   ├── py.sh             ← Cross-platform Python shim (Unix)
│   ├── py.cmd            ← Cross-platform Python shim (Windows)
│   ├── _lib/             ← Shared Python library package for all scripts
│   └── hooks/
│       ├── _policy.py      ← Shared write policy (write roots + name rules)
│       ├── guard_bash.py   ← PreToolUse: dangerous commands + redirection targets
│       └── guard_write.py  ← PreToolUse: write-scope boundary (roots + names)
├── WORKSPACE.md       ← Auto-generated: active projects + their inlined rules
└── CLAUDE.md          ← Claude Code entry point
```

## The three-IDE model

| IDE          | Role                               | Primary strength          |
|--------------|------------------------------------|---------------------------|
| Claude Code  | Planning, architecture, analysis   | Long-context reasoning    |
| Cursor       | Code implementation                | Inline edits, Agent mode  |
| Antigravity  | Refactoring, review, scoped tasks  | Focused transformations   |

Each IDE runs independently but reads the same rules and skills from `mesh/`.

## How rules and skills reach agents

1. `bin/sync_symlinks.py` (runs on every `SessionStart`) regenerates symlinks from
   `mesh/rules/` and `mesh/skills/` into each IDE's dotfolder — including
   `.claude/skills/`, which is Claude Code's native skill directory. All three
   IDEs therefore see the same flat skill namespace.
2. Rules with `alwaysApply: true` are injected automatically into every agent context.
3. Skills are on-demand playbooks — the agent loads the relevant `SKILL.md` when the
   task calls for it. A skill needs `name` + `description` frontmatter to be
   registered; without it, it will not be discoverable in Claude Code.
4. `.claude/projects-context.md` is a pre-inlined version of all active project rules,
   generated for Claude Code. Project and layer skills appear there as an index only —
   their bodies are not inlined, because `.claude/skills/` already makes them
   loadable on demand.

## Available slash commands

These commands are defined in `mesh/commands/` and available in all IDEs:

| Command | Script | Purpose |
|---------|--------|---------|
| `/add_project <name>` | `scripts/add_project.py` | Fuzzy-match registry, link project repo |
| `/remove_project <name>` | `scripts/remove_project.py` | Unlink a project repo |
| `/list_projects` | `scripts/list_projects.py` | Show all linked projects |
| `/workspace_status` | `scripts/list_projects.py` | Full workspace state overview |
| `/project_health` | `scripts/project_health.py` | Diagnostic health check across projects |
| `/git_backup [msg]` | bash (bin/) | Stage, commit, push workspace changes |

Always check `repos/_registry.yaml` before running `/add_project` — it lists all
available repos with their paths and tech stacks.

## Safety hooks (PreToolUse)

Two hooks run automatically before every tool call in Claude Code:

**`guard_bash.py`** is a **best-effort speed-bump** (defense-in-depth, not a security barrier).
It blocks common literal destructive shell commands including:
- `rm -rf` targeting system roots (`/`, `/etc`, `/home`, `/usr`, `/var`, `/bin`, `/lib`,
  `/lib64`, `/boot`, `/root`, `/sys`, `/proc`, `/dev`, `/opt`, `/srv`, `/sbin`),
  exact subpaths of `/home/<user>`, bare relative/parent/wildcard operands (`.`, `..`,
  `*`, `./*`), and home shortcuts (`~`, `~/`, `$HOME`)
- Destructive SQL (`DROP TABLE`, `TRUNCATE`)
- Force-push to `main`/`master`
- Low-level disk ops (`mkfs`, `dd`)
- `chmod 777`

Safe cleanup targets (`node_modules`, `./build`, `/tmp/...`, etc.) are explicitly allowed.

Heredoc bodies and quoted phrases are data, not commands: they are skipped
before matching. Every redirection (`>`, `>>`, `tee`) target is checked with the
same write policy as `Write`: heredoc/`echo`/`printf` content to a protected
name is denied, program output to one asks, and anything outside the write roots
is denied. `ln` into `projects/` or `mesh/` and write verbs (`sed -i`, `cp`, `mv`,
`rm`, …) on the guard files are denied.

**Honesty contract:** `guard_bash.py` reads the command text only and is
inherently bypassable (variable indirection, command substitution, a script that
writes files itself). It is not a security boundary. It **fails closed**: if it
cannot parse its input or crashes, it denies.

**`guard_write.py`** (Write, Edit, MultiEdit, NotebookEdit) allows a write only
when the **resolved** path is under a write root:
- the workspace, and every repo mounted under `projects/`, `mesh/layers/`, `mesh/mcp`
  (never `$HOME`, a disk root, a hidden home folder or a folder containing the workspace);
- `~/.claude/plans/`, this project's auto-memory, the session scratchpad
  (`scratchpad_dir`), and plain temp dirs (not `/tmp/claude-*/` harness state).

Inside the roots, on the path and on its symlink target, case-insensitively:
- **deny:** `.env*` (templates like `.env.example` are fine), any `.git` component,
  lockfiles; in the workspace, `WORKSPACE.md`, `mAIcelium.code-workspace`,
  `.claude/projects-context.md`, `.claude/skills/`, `.cursor/`, `.agents/`, `.antigravity/`
- **ask (the user confirms):** `.claude/settings*.json` of any project, `.sops.yaml`,
  key material (`*.pem`, `*.key`, `*.p12`, `*.pfx`, kubeconfig), and the guard files
  `bin/hooks/**/*.py`, `bin/py.sh`, `bin/py.cmd`

If a tool call is blocked, the reason says what to do instead: mount the repo
with `mai add <name> <path>`, write to the scratchpad, use the package manager,
or run `mai sync`. In `claude -p` or `dontAsk` mode an ask behaves as a deny.
Full model: `docs/security-model.md`.

## Changing mAIcelium itself (branch flow)

When the work is on the mAIcelium repo itself (not on a linked project), follow
`CONTRIBUTING.md`:
- branch from `dev` as `<type>/<slug>` and open the PR against `dev`; stacked
  work goes through an integration branch that also targets `dev`;
- `main` only receives the release PR `dev` → `main`. The `Branch flow` CI
  check fails any other PR into `main`;
- PRs into `dev` use `Refs #n`, and the release PR carries the `Closes #n`
  lines;
- after a release, fast-forward `dev` to `main`.

## Separated git mode

`bin/separate_git.py` moves `.git` out of the workspace root into a sibling directory
(`mAIcelium-git-backup/`). This prevents Cursor from treating the workspace root as a
git repo and showing all project files as uncommitted changes.

When separated git mode is active, all git commands on the workspace must use:
```bash
git --git-dir=../mAIcelium-git-backup/.git --work-tree=. <command>
```
The `/git_backup` command handles this automatically. Never run bare `git` commands
in the workspace root if `.git` is absent.

## Project lifecycle

```
# Link a new repo
bin/py.sh bin/add_project.py <name>        → creates symlink in projects/, updates WORKSPACE.md

# Remove a linked repo
bin/py.sh bin/remove_project.py <name>     → removes symlink, updates WORKSPACE.md

# Rebuild all mirrors after any mesh/ change
bin/py.sh bin/sync_symlinks.py             → regenerates .cursor/, .claude/, .agents/, .mcp.json
```

## What agents must never do

- Edit anything in `.cursor/`, `.claude/`, or `.agents/` — they are auto-generated.
- Run `rm -rf` on symlink targets inside `projects/` — that destroys the real repo.
- Create AI resources (rules, skills, commands) outside of `mesh/`.
- Act on projects not listed in `WORKSPACE.md`.
- Run bare `git` commands in the workspace root when separated git mode is active.
- Modify `WORKSPACE.md` or `.claude/projects-context.md` by hand — use `bin/` scripts.
